racula","html_url":"https://github.com/badsectoracula","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/badsectoracula/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-12-03T20:41:25Z","updated_at":"2024-12-03T20:41:25Z","body":"> I print a message about the invalid CSGNode and its node path.\r\n\r\nCouldn't we at least use the \"warning\" icon in the scene tree? That should stand out a bit more.\r\n\r\nI think this sort of debug message is incredibly easy to miss and may even be ignored. Imagine you are some level designer opening a level with 1000 meshes and ~150 CSGs used to \"cover\" seams here and there, often out of the way so they are not immediately visible but still contribute to the level's visual polish. And also imagine you have a bunch of extra plugins that for one reason or another dump stuff in the Output window (honestly even without any plugin here, that red icon seems to be on more often than not) so it is very easy for someone to simply learn to ignore the red icon until they notice something is wrong. It can help figure out why something is wrong once you figure it out (e.g. the path you mentioned) but IMO it might as well not be there before reaching that point.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2515509934/reactions","t